{"team":"BUF","col":"#4C8DFF","identity":"run-heavy","funnel":"run-funnel","plays_pg":61.7,"adot":7.9,"report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><h2>Built to Run</h2><p>The backfield runs through one man \u2014 <strong>James Cook</strong> absorbs <strong>66% of the carries</strong> (league ~59%), a true bell-cow share that makes his rushing volume among the safest floors in the sport when the script cooperates.</p><h3>The Offensive Blueprint</h3><p>They're a heavy under-center team (58% shotgun vs ~68%) \u2014 a pro-style, run-committed structure.</p><p>They run downhill \u2014 mostly inside the tackles (20% to the edge vs ~25%), a gap/power scheme that trades big plays for a steady, grinding floor.</p><p>By the numbers it's an efficient unit \u2014 a 48% success rate (above the 44% average).</p><h3>In the Red Zone</h3><p>Their red-zone identity is <strong>run-first</strong> (42% pass), funnelling touchdown equity to <strong>James Cook</strong>.</p><h2>Defense \u2014 Soft on the Ground</h2><p>They surrender <strong>126 rushing</strong> and <strong>209 passing</strong> yards per game (league ~120 / ~230). The run moves against them \u2014 <strong>target opposing RB1s</strong>.</p><p>They lock down opposing tight ends \u2014 just 24 rec yds/game (vs ~35); fade receiving props there.</p><p>They lock down opposing WR2s \u2014 just 28 rec yds/game (vs ~36); fade receiving props there.</p><p><strong>Soft against opposing WR1s</strong> \u2014 64 rec yds/game (vs ~58). A rec-yards target.</p><h2>The Betting Angles</h2><p>Bottom line: back <strong>James Cook</strong>'s rushing volume in favored/positive scripts; <strong>opposing RB1s</strong> smash on the ground and at the goal line.</p><p><em>Built on 2024\u201325 data; the profile rebuilds through the season as the current staff and roster set their own tendencies.</em></p></div>","depth":{"QB":[{"name":"Josh Allen","slot":"QB1","starter":true,"td":46,"props":{"Comp":19.73,"Att":30.19,"Pass Yds":227.43,"Pass TD":1.69,"INT":0.59,